  "articleBody": "Adding a kitchen is an exciting prospect, but there are definitely some common pitfalls folks fall into that you can easily avoid. One big mistake is underestimating lead times. People often forget to factor in how long it takes for design, getting approvals, and finding a good contractor who's actually available. You might think you can just jump right into construction, but those initial steps can take months! Another frequent error is skipping those crucial structural or planning checks. Even if you think your project is small, any change to the footprint or major internal walls usually requires a look-over. And don't even get me started on utility relocations! Moving gas lines or water pipes isn't a quick fix; it can significantly push your timeline. To avoid these headaches, it's smart to plan way ahead. Get at least three quotes and aim to book your contractor at least four months before you want to start. This gives everyone enough breathing room to get things right, from permits to ordering custom materials. If you’re not sure where to start, getting a professional opinion early can save you a lot of trouble and expense.",
